# Movie Booking Platform — Cursor Rules

## Project Context
This is a placement-portfolio full-stack project, not a startup MVP. The goal is
to demonstrate serious engineering (concurrency, caching, performance, testing,
documentation) through ONE cohesive system — not to add technologies for resume
keywords. Read PRD.md, architecture.md, and design.md before making structural
decisions.

## Tech Stack (locked — do not substitute without explicit user approval)
- Frontend: Next.js (App Router) + TypeScript + Tailwind CSS
- UI micro-interactions: React Bits (CLI-installed, source-in-project components only)
- Backend: FastAPI (Python 3.11+)
- Database: PostgreSQL (NOT MongoDB — this data is relational, use it)
- Cache / locking: Redis
- External data: TMDB API (movie catalog, posters, ratings)
- Deployment: AWS (EC2/ECS, RDS, S3+CloudFront, Route53, ACM)

## Explicitly Forbidden (out of scope for this project)
- Kubernetes, Terraform, Kafka, Jenkins, Prometheus — belongs to the separate
  DevOps portfolio project, not this one
- Microservices split — use a well-structured FastAPI monolith
- Redlock / multi-node distributed locking — single-instance Redis SETNX+TTL
  gate backed by a Postgres transaction is sufficient and more defensible
- Postgres read replicas — no justified write/read volume for this
- MongoDB, GraphQL — REST + Postgres is the correct fit here
- Any other animation/UI library besides React Bits — do not introduce
  Framer Motion standalone, Magic UI, HeroUI, etc.

## UI / Design Rules
- Light mode default. Base background white/off-white, primary text charcoal.
- Single accent color: purple (~#6C4FE0 family). Used for primary CTAs, active
  states, selected states, brand elements only — not decoratively everywhere.
- Status colors are functional only: green = success/confirmed, orange/red =
  error/failed/warning. Do not use gradients for status.
- Pill-shaped (fully rounded) navigation tabs, filter chips, date selectors.
- Cards: rounded-lg/xl corners, subtle shadow, white background, image + title
  + metadata stacked cleanly. No glassmorphism, no dark gradient hero sections,
  no default shadcn purple-gradient-blob backgrounds — these read as generic
  AI-generated UI and must be avoided.
- Seat map: small uniform rounded-square buttons in a tight grid, color-coded
  by state, with a legend row. Follow design.md for exact states/colors.
- React Bits usage should be sparing and purposeful — hover reveals, subtle
  press feedback, animated text on hero sections. Never add animation for its
  own sake; every use must have a stated UX reason in design.md.

## Backend / Concurrency Rules
- Postgres is always the source of truth for booking state. Redis is a fast
  gate/lock, never the final authority.
- Seat lock lifecycle: AVAILABLE → HELD (Redis TTL) → BOOKED (Postgres
  transaction) with automatic reversion HELD → AVAILABLE on expiry or payment
  failure.
- Every booking/payment-confirming endpoint must be idempotent (idempotency
  key on the request, not just at the payment-gateway layer).
- Any concurrency-critical code path requires an accompanying test before it's
  considered done — no exceptions.
- Handle and document these failure modes explicitly wherever relevant: Redis
  down, DB down, duplicate request, expired lock, partial payment failure.

## Coding Conventions
- Backend: standard FastAPI project layout — routers/, services/, models/,
  schemas/, core/ (config, security), db/ (session, migrations via Alembic).
  Business logic lives in services/, not in route handlers.
- Frontend: feature-based folder structure under app/ and components/;
  co-locate component + styles + tests where practical.
- Naming: snake_case for Python, camelCase for TypeScript/JS, PascalCase for
  React components and Pydantic/SQLAlchemy models.
- Every non-trivial function/endpoint gets a docstring describing purpose,
  not just parameters.
- Commit messages: conventional commits style (feat:, fix:, docs:, test:,
  refactor:, perf:).

## Documentation & Honesty Rules
- Never invent or assume performance numbers, benchmark results, or scale
  claims. If a metric hasn't been measured yet, use a clearly marked
  placeholder (e.g. `<TBD after load test>`), never a fabricated number.
- Any comment or doc claiming something is "production-grade," "scalable,"
  or "handles X users" must be backed by an actual test in the repo, or
  removed/softened.
- When in doubt about scope, prefer the simpler solution and note the
  trade-off in architecture.md rather than silently over-engineering.
